module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.ComputingIntersectionMultiplicities.HilbertPolynomialMultiplicity
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.TorFormula.IntersectionMultiplicity

/-!
# Positivity of a proper intersection multiplicity

Let `X` be a nonsingular variety and let `V`, `W ⊆ X` be closed subvarieties which intersect
properly. Then `e(X, V · W, Z) > 0` for every irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`.

The reduction to the diagonal rewrites `e(X, V · W, Z)` as `e(X × X, Δ · V × W, Δ(Z))`, where the
local ideal of the diagonal is generated by a regular sequence of length `dim X`; the
multiplicity formula for a regular sequence then exhibits that multiplicity as `dim X !` times
the leading coefficient of a Hilbert polynomial, and that coefficient is positive.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Intersection.ReductionToTheDiagonal`: the reduction to the diagonal, in the local form
  at the generic point in which the positivity argument consumes it — the Tor-formula
  multiplicity of a properly intersecting pair of ideals is the Tor-formula multiplicity of a
  pair on a second regular local ring whose first ideal is generated by a regular sequence.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegularSequence`: the Tor-formula
  multiplicity of a properly intersecting pair whose first ideal is generated by a regular
  sequence is positive.
* `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos`: the Tor-formula multiplicity of an
  arbitrary properly intersecting pair over a regular local ring is positive, given the reduction
  to the diagonal.
* `Stacks.Intersection.intersectionMultiplicity_pos`: the same positivity for the multiplicity
  `e(X, V · W, Z)` of two sheaves of ideals at a point of a scheme.

## Implementation notes

As for `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_leadingCoeff`, every
hypothesis is transported to the local ring `A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of an
irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`, where the multiplicity is defined: `I` and `J` are the
local ideals of `V` and of `W`, the vanishing of `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J))` says that `Z` is an
irreducible component of `V ∩ W`, and `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)` says that `V` and `W`
intersect properly along `Z`.

For a pair whose first ideal is generated by a regular sequence the positivity is unconditional:
the multiplicity formula computes it as `c !` times the leading coefficient of a Hilbert
polynomial of degree `c`, which is positive. A closed subvariety of a nonsingular variety need
not be a local complete intersection at `ξ`, and the reduction to the diagonal is what replaces
the pair `(V, W)` on `X` by the pair `(Δ, V × W)` on `X × X`, whose first member is cut out by a
regular sequence of length `dim X` because the diagonal is a regular immersion. That reduction is
carried as the explicit named hypothesis `ReductionToTheDiagonal`; it asserts an equality of two
Tor-formula multiplicities together with the two numerical conditions satisfied by the diagonal
pair, and asserts no positivity, so it is not an instance of the conclusion.

The second ring of a `ReductionToTheDiagonal` is quantified in the same universe as the first,
which the diagonal construction respects: `𝒪_{X × X, Δ(ξ)}` lies in the universe of `𝒪_{X, ξ}`.

## The two conditional statements below are not the route to Stacks 0B0V

`ReductionToTheDiagonal` quantifies over every regular local ring in a universe, with no ground
field, and that is more than the reduction to the diagonal gives, as is shown in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.ReductionToTheDiagonal.ReductionStrength`, where
`Stacks.Intersection.serrePositivity_cyclic_of_reductionToTheDiagonal` derives from it Serre's
positivity conjecture for a pair of cyclic modules — the conjecture
`Stacks.Intersection.SerrePositivityConjecture`, which this library states and leaves unproved.
So the hypothesis `h0B0U` of the two statements below is not dischargeable as stated, and they
are kept as they are.

Stacks 0B0V itself is proved, at the source's hypotheses and with no such hypothesis, by
`Stacks.Intersection.Diagonal.intersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegular`, which carries the
`@[stacks 0B0V]` attribute: there the reduction to the diagonal is available because `A` is the
local ring of a nonsingular variety over a field, so that `𝒪_{X × X, Δ(ξ)}` is a localization of
`A ⊗ A`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B0V](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0V)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B0U](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0U)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B04](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B04)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B0Q](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B0Q)
* [Serre, *Algèbre locale. Multiplicités*][Serre_algebre_locale]
-/

@[expose] public section

open AlgebraicGeometry

universe u

namespace Stacks.Intersection

/-- The Tor-formula intersection multiplicity of a pair of ideals of a regular local ring is
positive when the first ideal is generated by a regular sequence, the quotient by the two ideals
is zero dimensional and `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`: it is `c !` times the positive
leading coefficient of a Hilbert polynomial. -/
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegularSequence {A : Type u} [CommRing A]
    [IsRegularLocalRing A] {c : ℕ} (f : Fin c → A) (I J : Ideal A)
    (hf : RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    (hI : I = Ideal.span (Set.range f))
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hproper : ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)) :
    0 < I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) := by
  obtain ⟨P, -, -, hlead, heq⟩ :=
    localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_leadingCoeff f I J hf hI h0 hproper
  rw [heq]
  exact mul_pos (by positivity) hlead

/-- **Stacks 0B0U, in the local form at the generic point used by the positivity argument.** The
reduction to the diagonal: the Tor-formula multiplicity of a pair of ideals `I`, `J` of a regular
local ring `A` with `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` and `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)` is the
Tor-formula multiplicity of a pair `(span (range f), J')` on a second regular local ring `B`
satisfying the same two conditions, with `f` a regular sequence.

Geometrically `A = 𝒪_{X, ξ}` at the generic point `ξ` of an irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`,
`B = 𝒪_{X × X, Δ(ξ)}`, the sequence `f` cuts out the diagonal, which is a regular immersion of
codimension `dim X`, and `J'` is the local ideal of `V × W`; the equality of multiplicities is
`e(X, V · W, Z) = e(X × X, Δ · V × W, Δ(Z))`. -/
def ReductionToTheDiagonal : Prop :=
  ∀ ⦃A : Type u⦄ [CommRing A] [IsRegularLocalRing A] (I J : Ideal A),
    ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0 →
    ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J) →
    ∃ (B : Type u) (_ : CommRing B) (_ : IsRegularLocalRing B) (c : ℕ) (f : Fin c → B)
      (J' : Ideal B),
      RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular B (List.ofFn f) ∧
      ringKrullDim (B ⧸ (Ideal.span (Set.range f) ⊔ J')) = 0 ∧
      ringKrullDim B ≤ ringKrullDim (B ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f)) + ringKrullDim (B ⧸ J') ∧
      I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) =
        (Ideal.span (Set.range f)).localIntersectionMultiplicity (B ⧸ J')

/-- **Stacks 0B0V, in local form at the generic point.** Let `A` be a regular local ring and let
`I`, `J` be ideals with `dim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0` and `dim A ≤ dim (A ⧸ I) + dim (A ⧸ J)`. Then the
Tor-formula intersection multiplicity of the pair is positive.

Geometrically, for `A` the local ring of a nonsingular variety `X` at the generic point `ξ` of an
irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W` and `I`, `J` the local ideals of two closed subvarieties
intersecting properly, this is `e(X, V · W, Z) > 0`.

This statement takes the reduction to the diagonal, Stacks 0B0U, as the explicit hypothesis
`h0B0U`, so it establishes positivity only for a ring in which that reduction is already known.
It therefore carries no `@[stacks 0B0V]` attribute: Stacks 0B0V asserts positivity outright, and
a statement assuming the step its proof turns on is not that assertion. The second half of the
source's proof, which is unconditional, is
`Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegularSequence` above; the source's
other two inputs are the library's `Stacks.Intersection.isRegularImmersion_diagonal` (Stacks
0B0Q) and `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_eq_factorial_mul_leadingCoeff`
(Stacks 0B04), neither of which is assumed here. -/
theorem localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos (h0B0U : ReductionToTheDiagonal.{u})
    {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsRegularLocalRing A] (I J : Ideal A)
    (h0 : ringKrullDim (A ⧸ (I ⊔ J)) = 0)
    (hproper : ringKrullDim A ≤ ringKrullDim (A ⧸ I) + ringKrullDim (A ⧸ J)) :
    0 < I.localIntersectionMultiplicity (A ⧸ J) := by
  obtain ⟨B, _, _, c, f, J', hf, h0', hproper', heq⟩ := h0B0U I J h0 hproper
  rw [heq]
  exact localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos_of_isRegularSequence f _ J' hf rfl h0' hproper'

/-- **Stacks 0B0V.** Let `x` be a point of a scheme at which the local ring is regular and let
`V`, `W` be sheaves of ideals with `dim (𝒪_{X, x} ⧸ (V ⊔ W)_x) = 0` and
`dim 𝒪_{X, x} ≤ dim (𝒪_{X, x} ⧸ V_x) + dim (𝒪_{X, x} ⧸ W_x)`. Then the intersection multiplicity
of `V` and `W` at `x` is positive.

Geometrically, for `X` a nonsingular variety, `V` and `W` closed subvarieties intersecting
properly and `x` the generic point of an irreducible component `Z` of `V ∩ W`, this is
`e(X, V · W, Z) > 0`.

Like `Stacks.Intersection.localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos` above, of which this is the
scheme-theoretic reading, it takes the reduction to the diagonal as the explicit hypothesis
`h0B0U` and so carries no `@[stacks 0B0V]` attribute. -/
theorem intersectionMultiplicity_pos (h0B0U : ReductionToTheDiagonal.{u}) {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (V W : X.IdealSheafData) (x : X) [IsRegularLocalRing (X.presheaf.stalk x)]
    (h0 : ringKrullDim ((X.presheaf.stalk x) ⧸ IdealSheafData.stalk (V ⊔ W) x) = 0)
    (hproper : ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk x) ≤
      ringKrullDim ((X.presheaf.stalk x) ⧸ IdealSheafData.stalk V x) +
        ringKrullDim ((X.presheaf.stalk x) ⧸ IdealSheafData.stalk W x)) :
    0 < intersectionMultiplicity V W x := by
  rw [IdealSheafData.stalk_sup] at h0
  rw [intersectionMultiplicity_eq]
  exact localIntersectionMultiplicity_pos h0B0U _ _ h0 hproper

end Stacks.Intersection

end
